ll, including +17 from Quinnipiac and UNH in September", "Incumbent advantage for Lamont in a state that leans Democratic by eight points", "All seven expert ratings place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column", "Prediction markets price Lamont at roughly 96-97 percent"], "rationale": "Every recent poll shows Lamont ahead, most by 13 to 17 points, and the aggregated margin sits near 13.5 to 14. All seven professional ratings call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and markets price Lamont around 96-97 percent, leaving only a narrow path for Fazio.", "watch": "The lone co/efficient poll showed a seven-point race, so a consolidated Republican message on taxes and cost of living after the WFSB debate could tighten the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ned Lamont (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Connecticut governor Ned Lamont seeks another term against Ryan Fazio in a state that leans Democratic by eight points. Polling through the fall has shown Lamont with a steady double-digit lead: Quinnipiac and the University of New Hampshire each put him up 17 points in September, and aggregated averages place the margin near 13 to 14 points. Lamont has been on the air early with general-election ads and leads on the cost of living and the economy, the race's central themes.\n\nEvery major race rater, including Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, Sabato's Crystal Ball and the Silver Bulletin, calls the contest Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Lamont's chances around 96 to 97 percent. Fazio has campaigned on tax cuts and kept the race active on the air, but the polling gap has persisted all fall. The contest appears settled barring a major late shift.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 15.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9913, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.98